From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: USA-RedDragon <USA-RedDragon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:43:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(gateway): a carrier's API port relays until it exits, and
 a takeover does less per request

Under gateway.restart_turn_handoff_per_turn the old gateway's API port relays new connections to the next gateway's API server (another network namespace: the new pod). That relay was cancelled as soon as the last carried turn ended, while the process went on through its stop for a while: a client still routed to the old pod (its own containers, or the Service in the seconds after the pod went unready) got "Connection refused", and a request in flight on the relay was cut ("Remote end closed connection without response"). The relay now runs up to the end of the process (turn_carry.finish_api_relay, the last step of the shutdown tail): the port closes no earlier than API_ENDPOINTS_MARGIN_S (10 s) after the listener stopped accepting (the end of its relay window, from when the pod's readiness probe fails and the Service drops it), then each relayed connection ends at its next point between two HTTP exchanges (at once when idle) and one still busy after API_DRAIN_S (30 s) is cut (listener_relay.relay_api_listener, which follows each connection's requests and responses: Content-Length, chunked, 1xx, 204/304, upgrades). A gateway that is the pod's sidecar is its last container, so no client in the pod outlives the port.

The new gateway right after a takeover serves its own API clients, the old pod's relayed ones and the hub's reconnecting viewers at once, and some of that work was done more often than needed:
- every tracked mutation's end re-published the in-flight count on the event loop (a deep copy of the status snapshot, a PID record and a queued file write) even when the count had not changed; it is published only when it changes (status.active_agents_published);
- each CLI-surface session inventory (one per viewer that creates or resumes a session) scanned the skill trees again, because the scan bypassed the skill catalog cache to keep its Kanban view apart; the catalog now caches that view under its own key;
- the first resume and create after the takeover built the session socket's contract validators (pydantic defer_build) on the event loop; the standby builds them before the lock (standby_warm._warm_contracts);
- the log file handler formatted, and redacted, every record twice (once to decide on a rollover); it formats it once per emit;
- resolve_runtime_mode deep-copied the config to read agent.coding_instructions.
